{{- /*
  home.agentskills.json -- the Agent Skills discovery index.

  Publishes /.well-known/agent-skills/index.json alongside one republished
  /.well-known/agent-skills/<name>/SKILL.md per indexed skill.

  FOUR GATES, and the file is emitted only when all four pass:
    1. cfg.enable            -- the module master switch.
    2. cfg.skills_index.enable
    3. a NON-EMPTY resolved skill slice. With zero skills configured, or with
       every configured skill failing validation or its fetch, NO FILE IS
       EMITTED AT ALL. An empty JSON shell at a .well-known path is a claim
       of a capability that does not exist, and is worse than a 404.
    4. site.Language.IsDefault -- this format sets root = true, which pins
       ONE path for every language, so a non-default language would overwrite
       the default language's file. A multilingual site emits the index once,
       from the default language.

  THE `generated` KEY, emitted under cfg.skills_index.build_time (default
  true), is this build's timestamp from the same agent-readiness/build-time.html
  every other stamped surface reads, so it is byte-identical to every twin's
  `build_time`, to both link indexes' and about.md's `> Build time:` line, and
  to whatever a consuming site stamps its own surfaces with.

  It answers what the per-skill `digest` cannot. A digest says "is this
  different"; it never says "which one is NEWER", and a client holding a
  cached index needs the direction to know whether ITS copy is the stale one.
  That bites hardest here of all the module's surfaces: a stale index points
  an agent at a skill body it believes is current, and the digest beside it
  VERIFIES -- against the old bytes the client also cached. Index and body
  stay perfectly self-consistent and both are out of date.

  TOP LEVEL, not per skill: the document is published as a unit and its
  entries never come from different builds, so a field per entry would be N
  copies of one fact.

  THE ADDED KEY IS SANCTIONED BY THE CONVENTION, not merely unvalidated. The
  discovery draft states "Clients MUST ignore unrecognized fields" and says of
  the schema URI that it "does not need to be resolvable" -- so `$schema`
  stays EXACTLY as configured. It is an opaque version identifier there, and
  clients "encountering an unrecognized $schema URI SHOULD warn the user and
  SHOULD NOT process the index", which makes inventing a new value the one
  genuinely unsafe move available. See the README for the full decision.

  MEMBER ORDER IS CHOSEN, NOT INHERITED: `$schema`, then `generated`, then
  `skills` -- everything that DESCRIBES the document before the thing that IS
  it. A reader that samples a large JSON document from the front (a curl
  piped to head, a truncated preview, a streaming parser, an agent working to
  a context budget) then learns the format identifier and the build time from
  the first hundred-odd bytes, whatever the skill list weighs.

  That order is what a single jsonify call happens to produce today, and
  today only: jsonify serializes a Go map's keys ALPHABETICALLY, so the
  payload sits last purely because `$` (0x24) sorts ahead of letters and no
  key yet sorts after `skills`. One future key named `updated`, `version` or
  `warnings` would move the metadata behind the payload with nothing to say
  so -- which is exactly how the sibling search module's index came to carry
  its build stamp sixty-five bytes from the end of a quarter-megabyte
  document, where capable readers concluded three times over that it was
  absent. So the members are emitted one at a time, and `skills` is appended
  after the conditional block below rather than beside its siblings, which
  makes "the payload is last" a property of the code's shape.

  Every VALUE still goes through jsonify -- escaping, quoting and the nested
  indentation of the skill list stay the encoder's job (`prefix` is what
  indents that nested array to its parent's level), and the only characters
  written by hand are the braces, the colons, the commas and the three key
  names. noHTMLEscape is safe and smaller for a standalone .json file; this
  envelope must not be inlined into a <script> tag as-is.
*/ -}}
{{- $page := . -}}
{{- $cfg := partial "agent-readiness/config.html" (dict "page" . "site_only" true) -}}
{{- if and $cfg.enable $cfg.skills_index.enable site.Language.IsDefault -}}
  {{- $skills := partial "agent-readiness/skills.html" (dict "page" . "cfg" $cfg) -}}
  {{- with $skills -}}
    {{/* `with` has rebound the dot to the skill slice, so the page the
         build-time partial is handed comes from the capture above. */}}
    {{- $opts := dict "noHTMLEscape" true "indent" "  " "prefix" "  " -}}
    {{- $members := slice (printf `  "$schema": %s` (jsonify $opts $cfg.skills_index.schema)) -}}
    {{- if $cfg.skills_index.build_time -}}
      {{- $members = $members | append (printf `  "generated": %s` (jsonify $opts (partial "agent-readiness/build-time.html" $page))) -}}
    {{- end -}}
    {{- $members = $members | append (printf `  "skills": %s` (jsonify $opts .)) -}}
    {{- printf "{\n%s\n}" (delimit $members ",\n") -}}
  {{- end -}}
{{- end -}}
